Install and update (Steam Deck or a Linux gaming PC, from Desktop Mode)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
This bundle runs on a Steam Deck with stock SteamOS, and on a Linux gaming
distribution that ships umu-run. The game runs under Proton through umu-run
(umu-launcher): a stock Steam Deck does not have it, and the installer
offers to download it (see "umu-run" below).

Copy @APP@-@VERSION@-steamos.tar to the PC (USB stick, network share,
scp), unpack it (Dolphin: right-click > Extract > Extract archive here, or
`tar -xf @APP@-@VERSION@-steamos.tar`), open a terminal (Konsole) in the
unpacked @APP@-@VERSION@-steamos folder and run the installer. If
`./install.sh` says "permission denied" (an unpacker that dropped the
modes), run `bash install.sh` instead; the options are the same. The
unpacked folder can be deleted after the install.

  ./install.sh --steam      first time: installs to @DEFAULT_ROOT@ and adds
                            "@APP@" to Steam (Steam must be open)
  ./install.sh              every later version: installs on top, keeps the
                            last 3 versions, saves untouched
  ./install.sh rollback     back to the previous version
  ./install.sh status       what is installed, where the saves are, which
                            umu-run the game uses
  ./install.sh list         installed versions
  ./install.sh uninstall    removes the program; keeps hdd/, config/, logs/
  Options: --root DIR (default @DEFAULT_ROOT@), --keep N (default 3),
           --import-saves DIR (copy an old save root into an empty hdd/),
           --fetch-umu, --umu-dir DIR (below).

Play from Game Mode: the shortcut is "@APP@" in your library. Leave
"Force the use of a specific Steam Play compatibility tool" OFF for it: the
launcher starts Proton itself (umu-run, GE-Proton).

The first launch downloads the Steam Linux Runtime and GE-Proton (about
1 GB) and creates the Wine prefix, which takes a few minutes. A window
(or, in Game Mode, a notification) says so, and closes when the game
starts. Later launches are quick. The prefix (@DEFAULT_ROOT@/prefix) holds
nothing of value and can be deleted; it is recreated.

If Steam was not open during the install, add the shortcut by hand:
Steam (Desktop Mode) > Games > Add a Non-Steam Game to My Library > Browse >
@DEFAULT_ROOT@/@APP@.

umu-run
-------
The installer looks for umu-run on PATH, then in ~/.local/bin, then in the
copy it downloaded itself. When there is none (a stock Steam Deck), it asks
whether to download umu-launcher @UMU_VERSION@, the one release this bundle
pins, checked against its sha256; answer y. `./install.sh --fetch-umu` does
the same without asking. The copy goes to ~/.local/share/xboxrecomp/umu
(or --umu-dir DIR), in your home folder, so a SteamOS update keeps it, and
every game installed this way shares it. The launcher finds it there in
Game Mode too, whose PATH lacks ~/.local/bin.

Troubleshooting
---------------
- "umu-run is not installed" (a notification at launch, or from
  install.sh): run `./install.sh --fetch-umu` in the unpacked bundle
  folder, from Desktop Mode. `./install.sh status` shows which umu-run the
  game uses. UMU_RUN=/path/to/umu-run in config/launch.env picks another.
- Nothing on screen at the first launch: the downloads take a few minutes
  on a slow network. @DEFAULT_ROOT@/logs/umu.log shows their progress;
  the game's own log is the newest logs/game-*.log.
- The first launch failed half-way (no network): launch again; umu-run
  resumes. If it keeps failing, delete @DEFAULT_ROOT@/prefix and launch
  again.
- The notice window gets in the way: LAUNCH_NOTICE=0 in
  config/launch.env turns it off. It closes by itself when the game
  starts, or after NOTICE_MAX seconds (1200; set it there too).
- Steam's scaling filter (FSR): under gamescope (Game Mode) the launcher
  sets RECOMP_HOST_GAMESCOPE=1 and the game hands gamescope the frame at
  its internal resolution to scale. FSR applies only when that is smaller
  than the screen (render scale 1 on a Steam Deck).
  RECOMP_HOST_GAMESCOPE=0 in config/launch.env goes back to a window the
  size of the screen.
- Settings go in @DEFAULT_ROOT@/config/launch.env (KEY=value lines). The
  installer creates config/ and never changes what is in it.
- Proton's own log for a bug report: PROTON_LOG=1 in config/launch.env
  writes it to logs/ (steam-default.log). At each launch, a log in logs/
  over LOG_MAX_MB (64) is cut to its first and last halves; LOG_MAX_MB=0
  keeps them whole.

Controls
--------
Two controllers work, as players 1 and 2 (the split-screen modes): on a
Steam Deck its own controls are player 1 and a connected pad player 2.
There is no keyboard or mouse: the game's menus and races read only the
pad.

Burnout 3 on a Steam Deck
-------------------------
The game starts in a window: fullscreen is off by default
("fullscreen = false" under [present] in config.default.toml). Started
from Konsole in Desktop Mode, the Deck's controls are a mouse and a
keyboard, which the game does not read, and nothing quits it. So play it
from Steam: add it with ./install.sh --steam (or by hand, above), open the
shortcut's controller settings and pick the Gamepad template, and start it
from Steam, in Game Mode or Desktop Mode. Quit from the pad with the Steam
button, then Exit game.
